Every row lies below zero, so no weighting choice reverses the direction of the association, but its strength and the verdict on it change. The partial correlation runs from −0.195 to −0.325 across eight versions of a specification that any methods section would describe in the same sentence, and the two kinds of weight pull in **opposite directions**. Precision weighting by the respondent count weakens the result, from t = −3.80 to t = −3.04 in the conventional fit and from a bootstrap p of 0.110 to 0.206. Population weighting strengthens it, to t = −4.74 and p = 0.004. An analyst who reasons "I should weight, because weighting is more careful" without saying which argument they are making could end up with either.

Kish's correction changes almost nothing. With cells as interviewed, `n_eff` and `n_cell` are the same weight, because a cell mean that uses no survey weights has an effective sample size equal to its number of respondents, and with design-weighted cells they give t = −3.40 and −3.33. $n_\text{eff}$ is the correct precision weight, but the design effects in this panel are small enough that choosing it over `n_cell` does not change the conclusion.

The same holds across the curve. Swapped into every one of its 864 specifications, the respondent weight leaves 60.4% of them below zero with bootstrap p < 0.05 and the effective sample size 60.4%, against 70.9% on the curve itself, and each changes the class of 10.5% of the pairs. The curve keeps only the unweighted fit, because the precision weights rest on an argument that the error structure of these data does not support, and the population weight, which the next section takes up, answers another question.

## The average European

Weighting by adult population changes the quantity to the average European, in which the largest member state counts 215 times as much as the smallest. That is a defensible estimand, the one that a reader interested in the climate of opinion in the Union as a whole would want, but it is not the estimand of the claim. The claim concerns how a national economy shapes the framing of its citizens, so its unit is the member state, and the unweighted fit, in which Malta counts as much as Germany, is what a claim about national economies asks for. A result about the average European can therefore neither confirm nor undermine a result about the average member state, and it belongs in an analysis with its own estimand statement rather than among the robustness checks of the claim.

Across the curve the population weight leaves 99.5% of the specifications below zero, slightly more than the curve itself, but only 48.0% below zero with bootstrap p < 0.05, and it changes the class of 43.8% of the pairs. The fall in significance most probably reflects the weight itself, which lets a few large countries dominate the fit and leaves fewer countries to carry the evidence, while at the reproduced specification, as the table above shows, the same weight strengthens the result. The two estimands diverge in ways that a single specification does not reveal, which is a further reason for reporting the population-weighted analysis on its own terms.

## The denominator

When asked what the EU means to them, 26,325 respondents, 6.3% of the sample, mentioned nothing at all. How they are scored is a decision that no weight can change. The code of the original paper dropped those respondents, as the [missing answers module](missingness.qmd) explains, and so did two of the five Multi100 reanalyses, KQXUE and the reported model of PRL47. C6HJR, whose build produced the recorded t of −3.804, scored them 0, and the count and the mean of the five cosmopolitan items that KEVF1 and 018OL used score them 0 by construction. The course panel follows C6HJR, scoring those respondents **0** on all four framing scales, so a person with no opinion enters the data as a person with no cosmopolitan framing.

That would be a defensible convention if the no-opinion rate were the same everywhere, but it differs widely between countries.


