Without weights every respondent counts once, so the shares follow the numbers of respondents and the spread of their concern. Germany carries the largest share, 11.1%, and Italy, which took part in only two rounds, the smallest, 2.1%. The coefficient then describes the respondents as they were interviewed, the people whom the survey happened to reach in each country.

The design weight averages 1 in every country-round, so it rearranges respondents within each country and leaves the countries almost where they were: no share moves by more than 0.4 percentage points, and the coefficient goes from 0.175 to 0.177. Each slope now describes the adult population of its country rather than its sample, and the coefficient averages the national populations with weights that still follow the sizes of the samples.

Multiplied by the population weight, the design weight hands 76.9% of the estimate to the five largest countries, Germany, Italy, France, the United Kingdom and Spain, which hold 32.9% of it without weights. The slopes of those five differ widely. The United Kingdom has the steepest slope of all 16, 0.280, while Italy and Spain have two of the three flattest, 0.017 and 0.107, so the balance of the estimate shifts towards countries in which concern and distrust go together less, and the coefficient falls to 0.165. It now describes the adults of the 16 countries taken together as one population, Western Europeans rather than Western European countries or their samples. That population is pooled over the rounds of the survey, so a country counts once for every round in which it took part, and Italy, with two rounds, and Austria and Greece, with three, count for less than their populations alone would give them. The precision falls as well. With errors clustered by country the standard error rises from 0.0097 to 0.0113, because most of the evidence now comes from five of the 16 clusters.

### The weights on the curve {#sec-trust-curve}

The curve keeps three of the five options: no weights, design weights, and design weights multiplied by the population weight. Swapped into the 64 pairs of twin specifications, which agree on every other decision, design weights move the estimate by +0.001 on average (from −0.001 to +0.005) and population weights by −0.007 (from −0.016 to +0.008). No pair changes the verdict at the 5% level, and the weights carry 0.3% of the variance of the 192 estimates.

All three stay on the curve because the claim does not say which average it means. In the EU-frames case the population weight answers another question, as the section on [the average European](#the-average-european) explains, because that claim concerns national economies and its unit is the member state. The claim of this case is about individuals, as the first proposition of the paper puts it: "individuals expressing most concern about the impact of immigration on the national community will be most distrusting of politicians and political institutions" [@McLaren2012CulturalDivideEurope, p. 205]. Whether the average is to be taken over the respondents, over each national population or over Western Europeans as one population is left open, so each weight answers a defensible reading of the claim, and the curve holds them as three estimands rather than as three estimates of one.

The two post-stratification options stay off the curve as checks. The post-stratification weight describes the same national populations as the design weight, with a further correction for who took part, so it checks an answer that the curve already holds: at the reference settings it gives 0.1766 against 0.1772, and with the population weight 0.1630 against 0.1647. The SCORE replication of the claim on round 5 of the ESS weighted its respondents in this way and found 0.183, against 0.190 without the weights, a difference of the same order as those of @tbl-trust-five.

